.TH TULPAR-UPGRADE 1 "2026" "tulpar 0.1.0" "Tulpar Manual"
.SH NAME
tulpar-upgrade \- upgrade one or all installed packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tulpar upgrade
.RI [ options "] [" package [= version ]]
.br
.B tulpar u
.RI [ options "] [" package [= version ]]
.br
.B tulpar \-u
.RI [ options "] [" package [= version ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With a
.I package
argument, upgrades that single installed package to the newest
available version that satisfies it in the configured repositories.
Suffix the argument with
.RI = version
(e.g.
.IR foo=1.2.3 )
to pin the upgrade to that exact version instead of the newest one
available; this can also downgrade an installed package.
Without an argument, checks every installed package and upgrades all
that have a newer version available.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIpath\fR
Upgrade within an alternate root instead of the live system.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Skip the confirmation prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-require\-signature
Refuse to install any package whose signature cannot be verified.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " name"
Skip
.I name
during a full upgrade (no target package given). Repeatable. Only
valid without a
.I package
argument.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (including when there is nothing to upgrade), non-zero
if the transaction fails.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tulpar (1),
.BR tulpar-install (1),
.BR tulpar-hold (1)
